  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Two snapshots at common times of inertial frame K, with coincident origins at emission and K1 displaced right later. Light fronts move equally far from the fixed emission location C at speed c in K; the front clock recedes and the rear clock approaches that location. No c+v/c-v inertial light speeds or clock-rate claims.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-221.png",
      "sha256": "221ef4d9a5be1251de0a6d42d043a438a3d77c239d949e9b6f9a9ce36a43ae9d"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/title",
        "text": "同时的相对性"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0253#11+p0254#1",
        "text": "**信号和相互作用的传递不是瞬时完成的，传播速度存在上限**。\n狭义相对论中，这个上限是真空光速 c，各惯性参考系测得的真空光速相同。\n这些规律要求我们重新检查日常经验形成的空间和时间观念。\n\n经典的**绝对时间**观念把时间看成独立于物质及其运动、所有参考系共用的时间。\n它可以用于许多日常低速情形的近似描述，却不能继续作为精确而普遍的时空规律。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0254#2",
        "text": "先按经典的绝对时间观念，设想信号能瞬间传播。\n这样就可以直接比较空间两点 $A$ 和 $B$ 上的事件时刻。\n这是一个假想前提，不是现实中光信号的传播方式。\n\n在 $A$ 点和 $B$ 点各放一只在同一参考系中静止的钟，并用这个假想信号对表。\n例如，$A$ 处的钟指向 0 点 45 分时发出信号。\n\n如果信号到达 $B$ 点时，$B$ 处的钟也指向同一时刻，两钟就**同步**。\n如果读数不一致，就把落后的钟拨快，使两钟的读数相同。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0254#3",
        "text": "在选定的同一惯性参考系中，例如两次闪电击中不同地点，若事件发生时当地同步钟的读数相同，这两个事件就是同时的。\n比较的是事件发生时的钟读数，不是两道闪光到达某个人眼睛的时刻。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0254#4",
        "text": "可以在 $A$ 点和 $B$ 点各放一只同步钟，用当地的读数判断事件在这个参考系中是否同时。\n但真实信号的传播速度有限，怎样使相隔一段距离的钟同步呢？"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0254#5",
        "text": "同步时可以采用光信号或其他电磁信号，因为电磁波在真空中的速度是确定的常量 c。\n每个惯性参考系测得的真空光速都相同，在该系中向相反方向传播的光也具有相同的速度大小。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0254#6",
        "text": "无线电时间信号可以用来把家中的钟与准确的标准钟对准。\n知道无线电站到接收处的距离和信号传播速度，就能计算传播延迟。\n对表时应把这段延迟考虑进去，而不是把接收时刻当成发出时刻。\n\n在普通的日常对表中，附近电台的传播延迟往往很小，可以忽略。\n但精密计时不能随意忽略它，在遥远的宇宙距离中，信号传播所需的时间更是十分重要。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0254#7+p0255#1",
        "text": "图 220 给出一种利用对称性、不需计算传播延迟的同步方法。\n设宇宙飞船做匀速直线运动，两端固定着相同的钟 $A$ 和 $B$。\n在飞船中央放一个相对于飞船静止的光源，让它发出一次闪光。\n\n在飞船的惯性静止系中，光源到两钟的距离相等，光沿两方向的速度大小也相等。\n所以光同时到达两钟。\n\n若各钟在光到达当地时的读数相同，两钟就在这个参考系中同步。\n若读数不同，可以调整一只钟，使两次到达事件记录相同的时刻。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0255#3",
        "text": "图 220"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0255#5",
        "text": "图 221"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0255#6",
        "text": "上面的同时到达，是相对于飞船的惯性静止系 $K_1$ 而言的。\n在与飞船相对运动的惯性系 $K$ 中，按图 221(a, b) 所示的前进方向，飞船头部不断远离闪光发出的地点。\n这里比较的是光发出后的传播，不是要求光源继续留在原地。\n\n光到达头部的钟 $A$ 时，走过的距离大于这个参考系中飞船长度的一半。\n尾部的钟 $B$ 向闪光发出的地点靠近，光到达它时的路程小于这个长度的一半。\n光在两方向都以真空光速 c 传播，因此在 $K$ 中，光先到达尾部，后到达头部，两次到达并不同时。\n\n这不表示钟 $A$ 和钟 $B$ 的走时速率不同或发生故障。\n它表示在飞船静止系中同步的钟，按另一个参考系的同步规则比较时，并不同步。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0255#7+p0256#1",
        "text": "图示中，$A$ 点和 $B$ 点沿两系的相对运动方向分开。\n在 $K_1$ 中同时发生的两次光到达事件，在 $K$ 中并不同时。\n这说明，**空间分离事件的同时性依赖所选参考系**，不能把一个系的同时判断当成所有系共用的判断。\n\n根据相对性原理，惯性系 $K_1$ 和惯性系 $K$ 在物理定律上完全等效，没有哪一个更优越。\n它们可以对同时性作出不同判断，而各自在本系内的同步定义仍然一致。\n\n这里既用到了信号传播速度有限，也用到了各惯性系具有相同真空光速 c 的公设。\n不能只凭某一种信号传播较慢，就推出狭义相对论的同时性结论。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0256#2",
        "text": "同时性的相对性解决了第 94 节中球面光信号的表面矛盾。\n在惯性系 $K$ 中，取这个系的同一时刻，光到达以 $O$ 为球心的球面各点，这些到达事件在本系中同时发生。\n从相对运动的惯性系 $K_1$ 来比较，这同一组到达事件并不全都同时。\n\n沿相对运动方向处在不同位置的到达事件，其时刻不同。\n不能把原来的整组事件当成另一系同一时刻的球面波前。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0256#3",
        "text": "反过来，在惯性系 $K$ 中，以 $O_1$ 为球心的另一组球面到达事件也并不全都同时发生。\n这组事件在惯性系 $K_1$ 中才是同时的，情况与前一段交换两系后相对应。\n各系的球面波前都由各自同一时刻的位置组成，不是同一个普遍时刻下的球面。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/zh/p0256#4",
        "text": "因此，球面波前的描述并不自相矛盾。\n需要放弃的是所有惯性系共用同一时刻的经典假定，而不是各惯性系中相同的真空光速。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/title",
        "text": "Relativity of Simultaneity"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0253#11+p0254#1",
        "text": "**Signals and interactions do not travel instantaneously.\nThere is an upper limit to their propagation speed.**\n\nIn special relativity, that limit is the vacuum light speed c, and every inertial frame measures the same vacuum light speed.\nThese laws require us to reexamine the ideas of space and time formed from everyday experience.\n\nThe classical idea of **absolute time** treats time as independent of matter and its motion, and as shared by all frames.\nIt is a useful approximation in many everyday low-speed situations, but not an exact and universal law of space and time."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0254#2",
        "text": "First use the classical idea of absolute time and imagine that signals travel instantaneously, allowing direct comparison of event times at separated points $A$ and $B$.\nThis is a hypothetical assumption, not how real light signals propagate.\n\nPlace a clock at $A$ and another at $B$, both at rest in the same frame, and compare them using this imaginary signal.\nFor example, a signal leaves $A$ when its clock reads 0 hours 45 minutes.\n\nIf the signal reaches $B$ when the clock at $B$ reads that same time, the clocks are **synchronized**.\nIf their readings differ, advance the clock that is behind until the readings agree."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0254#3",
        "text": "In a chosen inertial frame, events such as lightning striking at two different places are simultaneous if the synchronized local clocks show the same time when the events occur.\nWe compare the local event times, not the times when the flashes reach someone's eyes."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0254#4",
        "text": "Synchronized clocks at $A$ and $B$ let us compare local readings to decide whether events are simultaneous in that frame.\nBut real signals travel at a finite speed, so how can separated clocks be synchronized?"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0254#5",
        "text": "We can use light or other electromagnetic signals for synchronization because electromagnetic waves travel in vacuum at a definite constant speed c.\nEvery inertial frame measures the same vacuum light speed, and light traveling in opposite directions within that frame has the same speed."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0254#6",
        "text": "Radio time signals can be used to set a household clock to an accurate standard clock.\nKnowing the distance from the radio station to the receiver and the propagation speed lets us calculate the signal's travel delay.\n\nClock-setting should allow for that delay, rather than treating reception as the instant of transmission.\nThe travel delay from a nearby station is often small enough to ignore in ordinary everyday clock-setting.\nIt cannot be ignored without considering the required precision, and signal travel time is especially important across large astronomical distances."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0254#7+p0255#1",
        "text": "Figure 220 shows a synchronization method that uses symmetry without calculating the travel delay.\nImagine a spacecraft in uniform straight-line motion, with identical clocks $A$ and $B$ fixed at its ends.\n\nA light source, at rest at the spacecraft's midpoint, emits a single flash.\nIn the spacecraft's inertial rest frame, the distances to the clocks are equal and light has the same speed in both directions.\n\nThe flash therefore reaches the clocks simultaneously.\nIf each clock shows the same time when the light arrives locally, the clocks are synchronized in this frame.\nIf the readings differ, one clock can be adjusted so that the two arrival events record the same time."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0255#3",
        "text": "Figure 220"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0255#5",
        "text": "Figure 221"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0255#6",
        "text": "The simultaneous arrivals above are defined in the spacecraft's inertial rest frame $K_1$.\nIn inertial frame $K$, which moves relative to the spacecraft, the craft's front moves away from the flash's emission location in the direction shown in Figure 221(a, b).\n\nWe are following the light after emission, not requiring the source to stay at its original location.\nBy the time light reaches the front clock $A$, it has traveled farther than half the spacecraft's length as measured in this frame.\n\nThe rear clock $B$ moves toward the emission location, so the light travels less than half that length to reach it.\nLight travels at the vacuum speed c in both directions, so in $K$ it reaches the rear first and the front later.\n\nThe arrivals are not simultaneous.\nThis does not mean that clocks $A$ and $B$ have different ticking rates or are faulty.\nIt means that clocks synchronized in the craft's rest frame are not synchronized when compared using the other frame's synchronization rule."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0255#7+p0256#1",
        "text": "In the example, points $A$ and $B$ are separated along the direction of relative motion.\nThe two light-arrival events are simultaneous in $K_1$ but not in $K$.\n\nThus **the simultaneity of spatially separated events depends on the chosen frame**.\nA simultaneous pair in one frame is not necessarily simultaneous in all frames.\n\nBy the principle of relativity, inertial frames $K_1$ and $K$ are equivalent in their physical laws, with neither frame privileged.\nTheir simultaneity judgments can differ while each frame has a consistent definition of synchronization within itself.\n\nThis explanation uses both the finite propagation speed of signals and the postulate that all inertial frames have the same vacuum light speed c.\nThe finite speed of just any kind of signal is not enough by itself to establish this special-relativistic conclusion."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0256#2",
        "text": "Relative simultaneity resolves the apparent contradiction about the spherical light signal in Section 94.\nAt a single time in inertial frame $K$, light reaches the points of a sphere centered at $O$, so these arrival events are simultaneous in this frame.\n\nIn the relatively moving inertial frame $K_1$, that same set of arrival events is not all simultaneous.\nEvents at different positions along the direction of relative motion have different times.\nThe original set cannot be treated as the other frame's wavefront at a single time."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0256#3",
        "text": "Conversely, in inertial frame $K$, the other set of arrival events on a sphere centered at $O_1$ is not all simultaneous.\nThis set is simultaneous in inertial frame $K_1$, corresponding to the previous case with the frames exchanged.\nEach frame's spherical wavefront is formed from positions at that frame's own common time, not at one universal instant."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n95/en/p0256#4",
        "text": "The descriptions of the spherical wavefronts are therefore consistent.\nWhat must be abandoned is the classical assumption of a time shared by all inertial frames, not the common vacuum light speed in each frame."
      }
    ],
